[patterns/application/api-microservices/PTN-APP-API-008-domain-driven-design-pattern.md#phase-3-migration-from-technical-services-to-bounded-contexts]

#### Phase 3: Migration from Technical Services to Bounded Contexts

**Step 8: Apply Strangler Fig Pattern**

Gradually migrate functionality from technical services (ValidationService, RulesEngineService) to bounded contexts (Claims Processing, Provider Management) without big-bang rewrite.

```yaml
Migration Strategy - Claims Bounded Context

Phase 1: Establish New Bounded Context (Weeks 1-2)
  Actions:
    - Create new Claims microservice with domain model
    - Implement Claim aggregate with core adjudication logic
    - Deploy alongside existing services (no traffic yet)
    - Set up database schema for Claims context

  Success Criteria:
    - Claims service passes unit and integration tests
    - Domain model validated with claims processors
    - Performance testing shows <200ms adjudication time

Phase 2: Strangler Facade (Weeks 3-4)
  Actions:
    - Create API Gateway routing rules
    - Route 10% of new claim submissions to new Claims service
    - Keep existing services handling 90% (fallback)
    - Monitor error rates and performance

  Code Example - API Gateway Routing:
    routes:
      - path: /api/claims
        methods: [POST]
        upstream:
          - service: claims-processing-v2  # New DDD service
            weight: 10                      # 10% traffic
          - service: claims-api-v1          # Legacy service
            weight: 90                      # 90% traffic

      - path: /api/claims/{claimId}/adjudicate
        methods: [POST]
        upstream:
          - service: claims-processing-v2
            weight: 25                      # Gradually increase
          - service: rules-engine-service   # Legacy
            weight: 75

  Success Criteria:
    - <0.1% error rate for new service
    - Response times within 10% of legacy
    - No increase in support tickets

Phase 3: Increase Traffic (Weeks 5-8)
  Actions:
    - Week 5: 25% to new service
    - Week 6: 50% to new service
    - Week 7: 75% to new service
    - Week 8: 100% to new service

  Rollback Plan:
    - Automated rollback if error rate >1%
    - Manual rollback if adjudication logic incorrect
    - Maintain legacy services for 3 months after 100% cutover

Phase 4: Decommission Legacy (Weeks 9-12)
  Actions:
    - Migrate historical claims data to new schema
    - Redirect all API calls to new Claims service
    - Decommission ValidationService, RulesEngineService
    - Archive legacy code

  Data Migration:
    - Bulk migration: 50M historical claims over 2 weekends
    - Dual-write: New claims written to both databases during transition
    - Validation: Automated comparison of adjudication results

Phase 5: Iterate (Ongoing)
  Actions:
    - Gather feedback from claims processors
    - Refine domain model based on new requirements
    - Extract additional functionality from remaining legacy services
```

**Real-World Migration Example:**

```csharp
// Legacy ValidationService (to be replaced)
public class LegacyClaimsController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpPost("api/claims")]
    public async Task<IActionResult> SubmitClaim([FromBody] ClaimSubmissionRequest request)
    {
        // Technical service approach (anemic model)
        var claim = _mapper.Map<ClaimEntity>(request);
        await _claimsDataService.InsertAsync(claim);

        // Call separate services for validation
        var validationResult = await _validationService.ValidateClaimAsync(claim.Id);
        var pricingResult = await _pricingService.PriceClaimAsync(claim.Id);
        var rulesResult = await _rulesEngineService.AdjudicateClaimAsync(claim.Id);

        // Scattered logic, no aggregate
        return Ok(new { claimId = claim.Id, status = rulesResult.Status });
    }
}

// New Claims Processing Bounded Context (DDD)
public class ClaimsController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly IClaimApplicationService _claimService;

    [HttpPost("api/claims")]
    public async Task<IActionResult> SubmitClaim([FromBody] SubmitClaimCommand command)
    {
        // Domain-driven approach (rich model)
        var result = await _claimService.SubmitClaimAsync(command);

        if (result.IsSuccess)
        {
            return Ok(new
            {
                claimNumber = result.Value.ClaimNumber,
                claimId = result.Value.ClaimId,
                status = result.Value.Status,
                _links = new
                {
                    self = $"/api/claims/{result.Value.ClaimId}",
                    adjudicate = $"/api/claims/{result.Value.ClaimId}/adjudicate"
                }
            });
        }

        return BadRequest(result.Error);
    }
}

// Application Service coordinates domain model
public class ClaimApplicationService : IClaimApplicationService
{
    private readonly IClaimRepository _claimRepo;
    private readonly IProviderClient _providerClient; // Anti-Corruption Layer
    private readonly IMemberClient _memberClient;     // Anti-Corruption Layer
    private readonly IFeeScheduleRepository _feeScheduleRepo;

    public async Task<Result<ClaimSubmissionResult>> SubmitClaimAsync(SubmitClaimCommand command)
    {
        // Validate provider (Anti-Corruption Layer translates Provider context model)
        var provider = await _providerClient.GetProviderAsync(command.ProviderId);
        if (provider == null || !provider.IsActive)
            return Result.Failure<ClaimSubmissionResult>("Provider not found or inactive");

        // Validate member eligibility
        var member = await _memberClient.GetMemberAsync(command.MemberId);
        if (!member.IsEligibleOn(command.ServiceDate))
            return Result.Failure<ClaimSubmissionResult>("Member not eligible on service date");

        // Create Claim aggregate (business logic encapsulated)
        var claim = Claim.Submit(
            claimNumber: GenerateClaimNumber(command.StateCode),
            providerId: ProviderId.From(command.ProviderId),
            memberId: MemberId.From(command.MemberId),
            serviceDate: DateOfService.FromDate(command.ServiceDate),
            lineData: command.ClaimLines.Select(l => new ClaimLineData
            {
                ServiceCode = ServiceCode.FromCpt(l.ServiceCode),
                Units = l.Units,
                ChargedAmount = Money.FromDollars(l.ChargedAmount),
                DiagnosisCodes = l.DiagnosisCodes.Select(DiagnosisCode.FromIcd10).ToList()
            }).ToList()
        );

        // Persist aggregate (triggers domain events)
        await _claimRepo.SaveAsync(claim);

        return Result.Success(new ClaimSubmissionResult
        {
            ClaimId = claim.Id.Value,
            ClaimNumber = claim.ClaimNumber.Value,
            Status = claim.Status.ToString()
        });
    }
}
```

**Migration Metrics:**

| Metric | Legacy (Technical Services) | New (Bounded Context) | Improvement |
|--------|----------------------------|----------------------|-------------|
| Deployment Frequency | Every 5 weeks | Every 2 weeks | Reduced cycle time |
| Change Failure Rate | Elevated | Reduced | Fewer cross-service regressions |
| Lead Time for Changes | 3 weeks | 1 week | Reduced coordination overhead |
| Mean Time to Recovery | Hours | ~45 minutes | Faster isolation of failures |
| Services Modified per Change | 3-4 | 1 | Single ownership per context |
| Lines of Code Changed | Complex multi-service changes | Focused single-context changes | Reduced cognitive load |

