[patterns/application/api-microservices/PTN-APP-API-008-domain-driven-design-pattern.md#tldr]

## TL;DR

**Problem:** Microservices decomposed by technical layers (Data Access, Business Logic, Presentation) scatter business logic across multiple services, create unclear ownership, and cause tight coupling requiring coordinated deployments. When business changes (e.g., new claims adjudication rule), developers must modify multiple services, coordinate extended release cycles, and experience elevated change failure rates.

**Solution:** Domain-Driven Design with Bounded Contexts - decompose microservices by business domains (Claims Processing, Provider Management, Member Eligibility), not technical layers. Each bounded context owns complete business capability with domain model (aggregates, entities, value objects), business logic encapsulation, ubiquitous language, and single team ownership.

**When to Use:**

- ✅ Complex business domains with 200+ business rules
- ✅ Monolith decomposition requiring clear service boundaries
- ✅ Cross-functional teams owning end-to-end business capabilities
- ✅ Frequent business rule changes requiring fast deployments
- ✅ Multiple product lines with distinct domain vocabularies
- ✅ Regulatory requirements mapping to domain boundaries (HIPAA, state-specific rules)
- ❌ Simple CRUD applications with minimal business logic
- ❌ Technical infrastructure services (logging, monitoring, caching)
- ❌ Small teams (<5 developers) without domain expertise

**Key Benefits:**

- **Business-Aligned Boundaries**: Services match business domains (Claims, Provider, Member), not technical layers
- **Single Ownership**: One team owns entire bounded context (domain model, database, API, deployments)
- **Reduced Coupling**: Contexts integrate through well-defined APIs and events, not direct database access
- **Ubiquitous Language**: Code uses business terms understood by domain experts
- **Independent Evolution**: Change Claims adjudication rules → deploy only Claims service (not 3-5 services)
- **Rich Domain Models**: Business logic encapsulated in aggregates with behaviors, not scattered across services
- **Faster Deployments**: Shorter deployment cycles vs coordinated multi-service releases
- **Lower Failure Rates**: Reduced change failure rate through single-context ownership
- **Regulatory Compliance**: HIPAA boundaries map to bounded contexts for PHI isolation

**Implementation Complexity:** High

- Strategic DDD requires Event Storming workshops, context mapping, ubiquitous language definition
- Tactical DDD requires implementing aggregates, entities, value objects, domain events, repositories
- Migration from legacy services requires strangler fig pattern over 3-6 months
- Team must learn DDD patterns and domain-specific business rules

**Prerequisites:**

- Domain expertise available (business analysts, SMEs who understand claims/provider/member domains)
- Business collaboration commitment (weekly Event Storming, domain model reviews)
- DDD training for development team (aggregates, bounded contexts, domain events)
- Executive support for 3-6 month migration timeline

**Quick Start - Define Bounded Context:**

```csharp
// 1. AGGREGATE ROOT - Encapsulates business logic

public class Claim : AggregateRoot<ClaimId>
{
    private readonly List<ClaimLine> _claimLines = new();

    // Domain properties (ubiquitous language)
    public ClaimNumber ClaimNumber { get; private set; }
    public ProviderId ProviderId { get; private set; }
    public MemberId MemberId { get; private set; }
    public ClaimStatus Status { get; private set; }
    public Money TotalBilledAmount { get; private set; }

    // Factory method (enforces invariants)
    public static Claim Submit(
        ClaimNumber claimNumber,
        ProviderId providerId,
        MemberId memberId,
        DateOfService serviceDate,
        List<ClaimLineData> lines)
    {
        if (!lines.Any())
            throw new DomainException("Claim must have at least one service line");

        var claim = new Claim
        {
            Id = ClaimId.New(),
            ClaimNumber = claimNumber,
            ProviderId = providerId,
            MemberId = memberId,
            Status = ClaimStatus.Submitted
        };

        foreach (var line in lines)
            claim.AddClaimLine(line.ServiceCode, line.Units, line.ChargedAmount);

        claim.AddDomainEvent(new ClaimSubmittedEvent(claim.Id, claim.ClaimNumber));

        return claim;
    }

    // Business behavior (not scattered across services)
    public AdjudicationResult Adjudicate(
        MemberEligibility eligibility,
        ProviderCredential credential,
        FeeSchedule pricing)
    {
        // Business rules enforced in aggregate
        if (!eligibility.IsActiveOn(ServiceDate))
            return AdjudicationResult.Denied(DenialReason.MemberNotEligible);

        if (!credential.IsValidOn(ServiceDate))
            return AdjudicationResult.Denied(DenialReason.ProviderNotCredentialed);

        // Apply pricing rules
        foreach (var line in _claimLines)
        {
            var allowedAmount = pricing.GetAllowedAmount(line.ServiceCode, line.Units);
            line.Approve(allowedAmount);
        }

        Status = ClaimStatus.Approved;
        AddDomainEvent(new ClaimApprovedEvent(Id, ClaimNumber, TotalAllowedAmount));

        return AdjudicationResult.Approved(TotalAllowedAmount);
    }
}

// 2. VALUE OBJECT - Immutable domain concept

public record Money
{
    public decimal Amount { get; init; }
    public string Currency { get; init; }

    public static Money FromDollars(decimal amount)
    {
        if (amount < 0)
            throw new DomainException("Amount cannot be negative");
        return new Money { Amount = amount, Currency = "USD" };
    }

    public Money Add(Money other)
    {
        if (Currency != other.Currency)
            throw new DomainException("Cannot add different currencies");
        return FromDollars(Amount + other.Amount);
    }
}

// 3. DOMAIN EVENT - Captures state change

public record ClaimApprovedEvent : DomainEvent
{
    public ClaimId ClaimId { get; init; }
    public ClaimNumber ClaimNumber { get; init; }
    public Money ApprovedAmount { get; init; }
}

// 4. REPOSITORY - Domain-language persistence

public interface IClaimRepository
{
    Task<Claim> GetByIdAsync(ClaimId claimId);
    Task<Claim> GetByClaimNumberAsync(ClaimNumber claimNumber);
    Task<List<Claim>> GetPendingClaimsForProviderAsync(ProviderId providerId);
    Task SaveAsync(Claim claim);
}

// 5. APPLICATION SERVICE - Orchestrates domain model

public class ClaimApplicationService
{
    private readonly IClaimRepository _claimRepo;
    private readonly IProviderClient _providerClient; // Anti-Corruption Layer
    private readonly IMemberClient _memberClient;     // Anti-Corruption Layer

    public async Task<Result<ClaimSubmissionResult>> SubmitClaimAsync(SubmitClaimCommand command)
    {
        // Validate via other bounded contexts (ACL translates their models)
        var provider = await _providerClient.GetProviderAsync(command.ProviderId);
        if (provider == null || !provider.IsActive)
            return Result.Failure<ClaimSubmissionResult>("Provider not active");

        var member = await _memberClient.GetMemberAsync(command.MemberId);
        if (!member.IsEligibleOn(command.ServiceDate))
            return Result.Failure<ClaimSubmissionResult>("Member not eligible");

        // Create aggregate (business logic encapsulated)
        var claim = Claim.Submit(
            ClaimNumber.Create(command.StateCode, 2024, GenerateSequence()),
            ProviderId.From(command.ProviderId),
            MemberId.From(command.MemberId),
            DateOfService.FromDate(command.ServiceDate),
            command.ClaimLines);

        // Persist (domain events dispatched)
        await _claimRepo.SaveAsync(claim);

        return Result.Success(new ClaimSubmissionResult
        {
            ClaimId = claim.Id.Value,
            ClaimNumber = claim.ClaimNumber.Value
        });
    }
}
```

**Common Pitfalls:**

1. **Anemic Domain Model**: Putting business logic in services instead of aggregates
   - ❌ Bad: `claim.Status = "Approved";` in ClaimService
   - ✅ Good: `claim.Approve(approvedAmount);` in Claim aggregate

2. **Aggregate Too Large**: Including too many entities in aggregate boundary
   - ❌ Bad: Claim aggregate includes Provider, Member, Fee Schedule entities
   - ✅ Good: Claim references Provider/Member by ID, loads via ACL when needed

3. **Direct Database Access Between Contexts**: Violating bounded context boundaries
   - ❌ Bad: Claims service queries Provider database directly
   - ✅ Good: Claims service calls Provider API through Anti-Corruption Layer

4. **Shared Database**: Multiple bounded contexts sharing same database schema
   - ❌ Bad: claims_db used by both Claims and Payment services
   - ✅ Good: claims_db for Claims, payment_db for Payment (separate schemas)

5. **Ignoring Ubiquitous Language**: Using technical terms instead of business terms
   - ❌ Bad: `ClaimEntity.UpdateTimestamp`, `GetById()`
   - ✅ Good: `Claim.AdjudicationDate`, `GetByClaimNumber()`

6. **Synchronous Cross-Context Calls**: Creating tight coupling between contexts
   - ❌ Bad: Claims service synchronously calls Payment service to schedule payment
   - ✅ Good: Claims publishes ClaimApprovedEvent, Payment subscribes and schedules asynchronously

**Real-World Results (Gainwell Claims Processing):**

| Metric | Before DDD | After DDD | Improvement |
|--------|-----------|-----------|-------------|
| Deployment Frequency | Every 5 weeks | Every 2 weeks | Shorter cycle time |
| Change Failure Rate | Elevated | Reduced | Fewer cross-service regressions |
| Lead Time for Changes | 3 weeks | 1 week | Reduced coordination overhead |
| Mean Time to Recovery | Hours | ~45 minutes | Faster failure isolation |
| Services Modified per Change | Multiple | 1 | Single context ownership |
| Test Execution Time | Extended | Faster | Context-isolated test scope |
| Lines of Code per Feature | Complex multi-service | Focused single-context | Reduced cognitive load |

**Governance Requirements:**

- **DDD Governance Board**: Approves bounded context boundaries, reviews context map changes
- **Context Ownership**: Each team owns entire bounded context (domain model, database, API, deployments)
- **Integration Standards**: Use Anti-Corruption Layer (default), Shared Kernel (exception with approval)
- **Breaking Change Notice**: 4 weeks notice before breaking API changes
- **Context Map Maintenance**: Living documentation of all bounded contexts and relationships

**Related Patterns:**

- [Database per Service Pattern](./database-per-service-pattern.md) - Each bounded context owns its database
- [API Gateway Pattern](../01-api-design/api-gateway-pattern.md) - Unified entry point for bounded contexts
- [Saga Pattern](./PTN-APP-API-018-saga-pattern.md) - Distributed transactions across bounded contexts
- [Event Sourcing Pattern](./PTN-APP-API-012-event-sourcing-pattern.md) - Store domain events as source of truth (advanced DDD)
- [CQRS Pattern](./PTN-APP-API-005-cqrs-pattern.md) - Separate read and write models within bounded context

**Further Reading:**

- Domain-Driven Design by Eric Evans (Blue Book)
- Implementing Domain-Driven Design by Vaughn Vernon (Red Book)
- Domain-Driven Design Distilled by Vaughn Vernon
- Event Storming by Alberto Brandolini

